            <p>When your mouth opens during sleep, airflow changes. Your body may still be “asleep,” but the quality of that sleep can be disrupted without you realizing it. That’s why so many adults wake up feeling unrested, tense, or dry, even when they’ve done everything “right” with their routine.</p>
            <p>Melatonin can help signal your body that it’s time for bed. But it doesn’t guide how your body behaves once you’re asleep.</p>
            <p>What more and more people are finding is that the smallest shift — one that feels completely natural — can have the biggest impact on sleep consistency... without changing evening routines or adding bulky devices.</p>
